## Your rights at work

PTSD can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life. If it does, your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- changes to duties that expose you to **triggers**
- a **quieter** or more **private** workspace, or a seat facing the door
- **flexible hours** and time off for **therapy**
- a **phased return** after time off
- an agreed plan for what to do if you feel overwhelmed at work

**Access to Work** offers a free **Mental Health Support Service**.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how PTSD affects you on **most days**. PTSD can affect:

- **mixing with other people:** feeling overwhelming distress, or needing someone with you
- **planning and following journeys:** not being able to go out, or only with someone you trust
- **preparing food, washing and dressing:** if you need prompting because of low mood, poor sleep or dissociation
- **managing money** and **managing treatment:** if you need reminders or support

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## Help for veterans

If your PTSD is linked to service in the armed forces, you may be able to claim through the **Armed Forces Compensation Scheme** or a **War Pension**. The charity **Combat Stress** and the **Royal British Legion** can help with claims.

If you cannot work because of your mental health, you may be able to get **Universal Credit** or **New Style Employment and Support Allowance**.
